WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.480
This video is brought to you by Nebula.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:14.040
Watch episode 2 right now by signing up With
the link the description.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:27.599
Late on a spring evening, 1992, the Endeavor
Space Shuttle, on its maiden flight, stood

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:30.820
on its launch pad in Cape Canaveral Florida.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.050
The crew sitting aboard ready to take on a
daring mission.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.590
A rendezvous between two spacecraft in orbit.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.270
A rescue mission.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.620
Bruce Melnick was aboard that day.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.660
The challenge we had was that this satellite
was never supposed to be touched by human

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:48.660
hands.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.590
Once it got an orbit, it was supposed to be
way up in geosynchronous orbit over 22,000

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.750
miles up.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.820
But it launched on an unmanned rocket that
failed to fire the second stage and send it

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.120
on its way.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.640
So it was stuck in a useless 800 mile high
orbit.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.619
Melnick and his crew mates were about to take
the ride of their life to rescue this satellite.

00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:13.420
To grab it with their bare hands and attach
a new second stage, but before that could

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.409
happen a complicated series of events was
about to take place beneath them.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:23.840
3 liquid fuel rocket engines were about to
burst into life with furious power that would

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.170
tilt the entire structure off axis,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:33.270
held back by just 8 explosive bolts, fed with
turbopumps driving fuel out of the external

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.229
fuel tank slung underneath the Orbiter.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.210
Meanwhile the two massive solid rocket boosters
lay dormant.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.070
The two largest solid rockets ever made to
date, and when the signal that the orbiter's

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:50.340
engines were up to the challenge arrived,
an inferno would be lit inside them, causing

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.979
the entire column of solid rocket fuel inside
to erupt.

00:01:54.979 --> 00:02:00.729
Lifting the revolutionary reusable space plane
off the ground and to orbit.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:07.920
This is the insane engineering of the Space
Shuttle

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.200
We are mere moments away from launch.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.730
Helium and nitrogen have purged the main engines,
the flight surfaces and main engine gimbals

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.090
have been tested.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:22.370
The Shuttle is now fully fueled and under
its own power, no longer connected to the

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.690
launch pad.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.580
Ten seconds are left.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.050
Sparkler-like igniters fire underneath the
engine to burn any stray hydrogen as the Shuttle

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.560
prepares to open the main fuel valve.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.870
Liquid hydrogen begins to race through the
fuel lines, pushed by the pressure of the

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:44.310
tanks, through the engines nozzles, where
it begins to expand and turn into a gas.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:49.489
This gaseous hydrogen drives a low pressure
turbopump turbine, elevating the rate of flow

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:52.450
of hydrogen through the fuel lines.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:57.560
This is all happening in the blink of an eye,
less than a second later the main oxidizer

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.620
valves open, unlocking the fuels true power.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:08.060
Fuel and oxygen meet in the two high pressure
turbopumps, raising flow rates even higher.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.510
Back in the nozzle we see our first flames.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:16.090
[REF] Oxygen levels are still low, and combustion
instabilities cause the engines to rattle,

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.560
but the engine is now ready to enter its next
stage.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:25.370
Entering open loop engine control, no longer
controlled by a prescripted start up sequence,

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:30.709
instead the engine constantly monitors pressure
readings and adjusts valves accordingly.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:36.790
The engine begins to throttle up, oxygen valves
continually opening wider, the flame transitions

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.370
from a lower pressure orange flame to an eerie
hot blue.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:46.120
The Space Shuttle is unique in that these
engines are not directly under the fuel tanks.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.410
The expensive rocket engines were attached
to the reusable shuttle, not the expendable

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:52.489
external tank.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:57.150
And because the main engines are offset from
the solid rocket boosters, which are holding

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.849
you to the pad, when we light off the main
engines at seven seconds, it actually bends

00:04:01.849 --> 00:04:02.989
that stack over.

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:06.129
And you can feel this little lurching back
and forth.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:07.439
NASA calls it twang.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:09.290
kind of a real technical term.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.670
The only thing stopping the entire shuttle
from tipping over at this point

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:19.400
are the hold down posts, attached to the solid
rocket boosters, which for now lay dormant,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.560
waiting for the signal to ignite.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.910
The pressure inside the diverging nozzle is
still lower than atmospheric pressure, and

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:32.470
we can see a flow separation layer, which
gradually creeps to the edge of the nozzle

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.720
as the pressure inside increases.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.009
3 seconds until launch.

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:41.940
The engines are checked to ensure they are
running at 90% of their rated thrust.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.100
The time has come for the solid rocket boosters
to ignite.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:51.600
The solid rocket boosters are 45 meters long
and are filled to the brim with solid rocket

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:58.860
fuel, a rubbery mixture that combines both
the fuel and oxidiser into one solid material.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.070
The oxidizer, ammonium perchlorate, makes
up nearly 70% of the mixture, while 16 percent

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.360
was the aluminium fuel

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.470
We need a way to ignite the entire column,
quickly.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:17.009
The igniter itself was essentially a miniature
solid rocket motor, which itself was ignited

00:05:17.009 --> 00:05:20.580
by an even smaller miniature solid rocket
motor.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.020
To ensure extremely quick combustion, this
main igniter was shaped with a 40 point star

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.139
orifice.

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:31.920
When it comes to solid rocket motors, the
more surface area available to burn, the quicker

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.419
it will burn, rapidly producing the heat and
pressure needed to ignite the entire column

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:39.300
of solid propellant.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.919
Once this happened there was no turning back
so there were several safety mechanisms in

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:47.620
the arming sequence to prevent it happening
accidentally.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:52.360
The first part of the arming sequence began
when technicians accessed the safety and arming

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.360
device

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.039
in the top skirt of the solid rocket booster
here to remove a pin.

00:05:58.039 --> 00:06:03.150
This pin locked a safety device in place,
preventing it from rotating into an armed

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.060
position by accident.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:11.050
When it was rotated into position on command,
at t minus 5 minutes, it connected a standard

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:14.990
NASA detonator through a tunnel to the igniter
system.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:19.960
When it fires, it begins the rapid chain of
events that brings the space shuttle to its

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:26.740
highest thrust, but the entire rocket is still
anchored to the launch pad, held down by the

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.730
hold down posts, 4 on each solid rocket booster.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:36.480
These hold down posts anchor the solid rockets
to the ground through the aft skirt with massive

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.410
711 millimeter long and 88 millimeter wide
bolts.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:48.180
The lower side of the bolt is tensioned using
a regular nut, while the upper nut is a special

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.240
kind of explosive nut, a frangible nut.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.240
[FOOTAGE]

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.289
On two occasions (STS-2 and STS-92) two of
these frangible nuts did not fracture correctly,

00:06:56.289 --> 00:06:59.440
something NASA described as stud hang ups.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.569
These issues did not stop the space shuttle
from taking off, with the power of two solid

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:09.889
rocket boosters and 3 main engines one or
two improperly fractured nuts were not going

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:12.740
to stop the Space Shuttle from taking off.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.849
These hold down posts were primarily designed
to prevent the space shuttle from tipping

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:22.300
over while the 3 main engines were brought
up to full throttle, or simply by a gust of

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:23.599
wind.

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:29.639
However if 4 or more stud hang ups occurred
the issue could have been mission critical,

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:36.639
and so a redesign was introduced for STS-126,
where a crossover assembly was included.

00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:42.039
This new system linked the two pyrotechnic
charges in each nut to ensure they detonated

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:44.569
within 50 microseconds of each other.

00:07:44.569 --> 00:07:51.460
A delay of up to 250 microseconds was possible
with the previous design and this was identified

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.750
as the main issue causing the so-called stud
hang ups.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:55.750
[REF]

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:00.550
The shuttle is now free, with the largest
and most powerful solid rockets ever used

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.840
in human space flight powering it upwards.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.920
Each of the solid rocket boosters provided
13,000 kilonewtons of thrust each, nearly

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.060
double that of the Falcon 9 first stage (7.6
MN)

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:18.200
But the very notion of using solid rocket
boosters for a crewed space shuttle was initially

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.699
met with extreme concern.

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:25.310
Using solid rocket boosters for human space
flight had never been attempted before.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:30.780
They were nearly exclusively used for military
use, like the minuteman ICBM.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.490
The solid fuel allowing them to stand ready,
available to launch in a moment's notice.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:41.550
However, once ignited there is little that
can be done to stop them, the entire column

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.880
of propellant is ignited at once.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:49.450
There are no pumps or valves that can lower
the fuel and oxidiser being delivered.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:54.370
Once ignited, they stay ignited until all
of the propellant is expended.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:09:00.560
This, ofcourse, comes with extreme control
issues that need to be engineered around.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.370
To begin, the engine needs to be throttled
back as it expends fuel.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.660
As fuel is used the weight of the rockets
lowers.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:14.730
If the thrust isn’t lowered the rocket could
exceed its max allowable dynamic pressure

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:16.560
and disintegrate.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.370
An improperly designed fuel column could make
matters even worse, as a simple circular internal

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:27.720
cavity will result in an increase in thrust
as the fuel is burned.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:33.010
However, many different thrust profiles can
be achieved with different shapes.

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:38.470
A pointed star cavity will start with higher
thrust, thanks to the increased surface area,

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:44.560
but rapidly decrease in thrust, as the points
of the star burn down and the surface area

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:45.560
decreases.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.560
This effect is increased by increasing the
number of points on the star, which is why

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:52.959
the igniter had 40.

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:57.700
Nearly every illustration online shows the
Space Shuttle Solid Rocket boosters with a

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.660
continuous cylinder

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:04.100
If this was the design of the fuel channel,
it could have caused issues.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:10.450
The surface area of a circular internal cavity
would have increased as fuel was burned, because

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:14.670
the cavity's diameter and circumference would
be increasing.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:18.500
This is not how the solid rocket boosters
were designed.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.660
The upper section of the solid rocket booster
had an 11 pointed star shape.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:28.790
This shape was created with this mandrel placed
inside the upper section before the fuel mixture

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:30.170
was poured in.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:35.950
This shape then transitioned into a circular
section, however it wasn’t constant in diameter.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:39.279
The diameter increased toward the nozzle.

00:10:39.279 --> 00:10:45.290
This design provided elevated thrust at ignition,
when it was needed most, and then reduced

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:50.610
gradually to lower the maximum dynamic pressure
the space shuttle experienced, with the thrust

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:55.060
decreasing by 33% 50 seconds into flight.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:11:00.260
When filling the solid rocket booster segments
with the propellant they were filled in pairs,

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.760
matching with its contemporary segment on
the other booster.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:09.310
This allowed the two segments to be filled
using the same batch of fuel mixture, so if

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:14.670
there were any inconsistencies in the mix
they would be at the very least consistent

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:20.110
on the left and right side of the shuttle,
ensuring differential thrust from the boosters

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:21.920
would not drive it ofcourse.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:27.461
However, all 5 of the nozzles are capable
of thrust vectoring to control the Space Shuttle

00:11:27.461 --> 00:11:34.769
during ascent and 80% of the steering power
came from the SRBs, thanks to their power

00:11:34.769 --> 00:11:39.000
and increased distance from the central axis
of the shuttle.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.579
The Space Shuttles aerodynamic flight surfaces
played no part in control during ascent, but

00:11:44.579 --> 00:11:49.360
they did actuate to reduce aerodynamic stress
and drag.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:55.680
7 seconds after take off, with the Space Shuttle
traveling around 140 kilometers per hour (87

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.160
mph), a roll programme is initiated, through
asymmetric thrust vectoring of the nozzles

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:01.820
of the solid rocket boosters [Footage]

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:05.579
with actuators mounted inside the aft skirt
here.

00:12:05.579 --> 00:12:10.550
There are 2 hydraulic actuators on each nozzle
to allow it to move in both pitch and yaw.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:17.150
The space shuttle turns over 180 degrees and
pitches 12 degrees off a straight up trajectory,

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:21.610
placing the Shuttle on a heading towards its
assigned orbit, lowering the dynamic pressure

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:26.410
on the shuttle's wings, and providing the
flight crew a view of the horizon out of their

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:27.410
windows.

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:28.410
[Footage]

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:32.580
Ofcourse, this 180 degree rotation would not
have been needed if the Space Shuttle was

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:37.779
simply rotated on the launch pad, but the
launch pad was designed for the Saturn V.

00:12:37.779 --> 00:12:42.839
The position of the service tower, and all
the supporting infrastructure associated with

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:47.730
it, locked the Space Shuttle into this orientation,
and it was simply easier to program a roll

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.690
than to build a new launch pad.

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:53.649
45 seconds into the flight something fascinating
happens.

00:12:53.649 --> 00:12:58.010
The space shuttle exceeds the speed of sound,
and the sound of those rocket engines can

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.410
no longer reach the astronauts.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:03.780
So you don't hear those rocket motors that
much anymore behind you.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:08.190
But now you hear the air rushing down the
side of the shuttle in a thickest part of

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:12.720
the atmosphere, and it sounds like a train
howling through a tunnel when that air is

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.459
going across the rough blankets and tiles
on the outside of the shuttle.

00:13:16.459 --> 00:13:20.660
And as the space shuttle races higher and
higher into the atmosphere and the air gets

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:25.079
progressively thinner, even this sound begins
to dwindle away.

00:13:25.079 --> 00:13:30.399
for the last 1520 seconds of first stage,
it's just a quiet, very rough ride because

00:13:30.399 --> 00:13:32.810
you're still on the solid rocket boosters

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:36.750
And it will get even quieter as the solid
rocket boosters reach the end of their fuel

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.639
supply 2 minutes into the flight.

00:13:39.639 --> 00:13:44.090
Lowering chamber pressures trigger sensors
to begin the separation process, however if

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:49.070
these fail a backup system based simply on
time elapsed takes over.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:53.040
Once triggered the thrust vectoring system
is commanded to return the nozzles to the

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:58.529
neutral position and the orbiter is commanded
to maintain its attitude for 4 seconds.

00:13:58.529 --> 00:14:02.029
The SRBs are attached to the external tank
at 4 points.

00:14:02.029 --> 00:14:06.760
Through 3 struts on the lower section of the
rocket and an additional upper connection,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:10.800
each held in place by frangible connections
that are severed on command.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:15.540
At the same time 8 separation motors, 4 on
top of the solid rocket booster and 4 on the

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:17.149
bottom, fire.

00:14:17.149 --> 00:14:22.009
These were also solid motors and provided
over 96 thousand newtons of thrust, ensuring

00:14:22.009 --> 00:14:27.440
the SRBs safely detached from the space shuttle
as it continued its flight to orbit.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.639
This solid rocket booster separation process
was the thing that caught Bruce off guard

00:14:31.639 --> 00:14:34.500
most on his first flight.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.980
picture this, you're sitting on top of the
explosive bolts that are holding the boosters

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.570
to the tank.

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:45.029
So when those explosive bolts let go, it's
a large bang.

00:14:45.029 --> 00:14:49.010
And then the booster separation motors, which
are little rocket motors that fly the boosters

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:55.040
away from the shuttle so it doesn't recontact
it, those exhausts are aimed right at your

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:56.259
windshield.

00:14:56.259 --> 00:15:01.420
So it's staging instead of there being this
little pop and off you go in second stage,

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:07.009
it's this big bang, and then you're in this
fireball where the exhaust from the booster

00:15:07.009 --> 00:15:12.019
sep motors are over the windshield, and it's
like, geez, did we just blow up?

00:15:12.019 --> 00:15:17.890
And then very quickly you get through that
and you can see the nice dark of space ahead

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:18.890
of you.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:22.829
So that's really the only surprise going uphill
that I had on my first flight.

00:15:22.829 --> 00:15:28.410
At this point the SRBs are continuing to climb
in altitude, just slower than the Space Shuttle,

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.460
upon reaching their apogee, roughly a minute
later they will begin their journey back down

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:38.009
to earth, eventually releasing a series of
parachutes from its nose cone to slow descent

00:15:38.009 --> 00:15:41.000
and land in the ocean to be recovered and
reused.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:46.389
Meanwhile the Orbiter is continuing its journey
in its second stage of flight.

00:15:46.389 --> 00:15:48.199
Second stage is smooth.

00:15:48.199 --> 00:15:52.720
Matter of fact, most astronauts will call
it electric drive, but now is when the GS

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.779
start to build up and you're getting pushed
further and further back in your seat.

00:15:56.779 --> 00:16:01.509
And then we throttle the engines back when
we reach three Gs because it is a winged vehicle.

00:16:01.509 --> 00:16:04.500
So there's a lot of aerodynamics going on
there.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:09.740
And for the structural integrity, we go ahead
and throttle back to three Gs because the

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.310
wings could come off if you accelerated a
whole lot more than that, and that would make

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:14.959
for a bad day.

00:16:14.959 --> 00:16:19.800
Under the power of the 3 main engines, fed
with liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen from

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.860
the external tank, the shuttle continues its
way to orbit.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:28.519
This external tank is a bit of an anomaly,
the Space Shuttle was intended to be fully

00:16:28.519 --> 00:16:34.290
reusable when it was first conceived, with
the fuel and oxidiser tanks incorporated into

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.560
the orbiter itself.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:39.360
In fact, the Space Shuttle was envisioned
in many different forms

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:44.980
Ultimately funding challenges forced the Space
Shuttle’s ambitious designs to be simplified,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:50.060
and the Air Force was approached to bolster
funds influencing the design to stray even

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:52.730
further from the original vision.

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:57.519
Primarily in an increased cargo bay capacity
and increased glide range.

00:16:57.519 --> 00:17:01.589
The external tank was however the largest
compromise of design.

00:17:01.589 --> 00:17:05.539
Gone was the idea of a manned external tank
that would glide back to land.

00:17:05.539 --> 00:17:10.579
What would have saved money in the long run
simply would have been too expensive to develop.

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:13.470
Relying instead on an extendable external
tank.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:18.650
A massive non-reusable structure that would
disintegrate during reentry.

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:20.510
The external was heavy.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:26.030
It wasn’t simply two pressurized tanks,
it was a structurally integral component,

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:31.030
responsible for transmitting enormous loads,
it was the centerpiece of the space shuttle

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:32.030
system.

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:33.490
The structural backbone.

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:37.059
Connecting to both the solid rocket boosters
and the orbiter.

00:17:37.059 --> 00:17:40.549
In order to do this the external tank had
to be strong.

00:17:40.549 --> 00:17:46.160
It was 47 meters long and weighed 33.5 metric
tonnes, empty.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.380
Its aluminum skin is reinforced with strengthening
rings and stringers.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:55.110
This was essentially a massive thermos flask
with a spaced double skin to insulate the

00:17:55.110 --> 00:18:00.280
cryogenic fuel inside, finally the entire
structure was covered in a layer of orange

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:04.320
insulation foam, which alone weighed 2.2 tonnes.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:09.760
The first two space shuttle missions featured
a white external tank, coated in a fire retardant

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:13.409
latex, which was removed to save a quarter
tonne of weight.

00:18:13.409 --> 00:18:16.990
The liquid oxygen tank was located on top
of the external tank.

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:22.130
It’s much smaller than the liquid hydrogen
tank thanks to oxygen’s much higher density,

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:26.690
and this is part of why the liquid oxygen
tank was placed on top of the external tank,

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:31.280
as it pushes the rocket's center of gravity
higher, an important aspect of stability in

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:32.320
flight.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:37.440
If a rocket is traveling straight up drag
and lift acts through the center of pressure.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:41.660
The location of the center of pressure is
determined by the location of aerodynamic

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:47.480
surfaces and engines, but the rocket rotates
around the center of gravity, which is determined

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:52.730
by the placement of mass, because of this
it makes sense to place weight higher on the

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:56.090
rocket, as it moves the center of gravity
above the center of pressure.

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:57.090
[REF]

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:01.321
Imagine a rocket flying with its orientation
aligned with its direction of flight, when

00:19:01.321 --> 00:19:07.169
a gust of wind knocks it off balance it introduces
an angle of attack, which creates lift acting

00:19:07.169 --> 00:19:11.650
through the center of pressure, and the rocket
rotates around the center of gravity.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:14.110
Returning the rocket to its original position.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:18.700
If the center of gravity was below the center
of pressure the opposite would happen and

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.350
the rocket would be unstable.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:25.480
So, the dense liquid oxidiser was placed on
top of the external tank.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:29.770
This came with the added benefit of allowing
the inter tank to be placed higher on the

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:33.200
external tank, as the hydrogen tank is much
larger.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:37.200
The intertank is a load bearing structure
between the two tanks.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:42.100
There is even a massive beam hidden within
this intertank resisting enormous compressive

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:46.220
loads as the solid rocket motors attempt to
push inwards.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:50.490
The lower support strut holding the solid
rocket boosters takes all of the shear stress

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:55.700
created by the upwards thrust of the booster,
and so the upper support acts primarily in

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:56.890
compression.

00:19:56.890 --> 00:20:01.730
The forward attachment point to the orbiter
is also located on the lower portion of the

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:02.730
intertank.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:03.730
[REF]

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:07.390
Ofcourse, all of this liquid oxidiser and
fuel needed to get to the main shuttle engines

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:08.730
on the orbiter.

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:12.809
The liquid oxygen exits the tank here, this
pipe is massive.

00:20:12.809 --> 00:20:14.869
46 centimeters wide.

00:20:14.869 --> 00:20:16.520
Big enough to crawl through.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.919
When the liquid oxygen begins to flow, it
acts much like water, and with this rate of

00:20:20.919 --> 00:20:23.960
flow a massive whirlpool could form.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:28.179
With the weight of all this fluid swirling
and sloshing inside the tank this could cause

00:20:28.179 --> 00:20:33.400
massive instability issues, or it could even
expose the outlet and allow bubbles to enter

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.450
the engine.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:39.160
So an anti-vortex and anti-sloshing baffles
are placed on the bottom of the tank where

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:41.180
the oxidiser exits.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:45.380
Liquid oxygen travels down this pipe into
the interstage where it exits and runs along

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:50.950
the length outside of the liquid hydrogen
tank until it meets the primary orbiter connection

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:51.960
point.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:56.620
This connection point connects physically
to the orbiter, but is also the primary connection

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.630
for the liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:03.770
To the left and right of this truss connection
are two umbilical plates.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:08.370
Which mated directly to corresponding plates
on the underside of the orbiter.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:13.010
The left plate provided a connection for liquid
hydrogen and the right plate provided a connection

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:14.500
for liquid oxygen.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.529
Each also had gaseous lines that brought back
gaseous hydrogen and oxygen from the main

00:21:19.529 --> 00:21:21.600
engine to their respective tanks.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.760
This was vital in order to maintain ullage
pressure.

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:30.600
Ullage is the volume of gas above a liquid
storage tank, like the cryogenic liquid oxidiser

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.140
and hydrogen tanks.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:36.549
The pressure of this volume has to be maintained
as the liquid lowers.

00:21:36.549 --> 00:21:42.000
If the pressure is allowed to lower the structural
integrity of the tanks could be compromised.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:46.530
The gaseous hydrogen used to pressurize the
tank was tapped off from the hydrogen fuel

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:51.700
line after it went through the regenerative
cooling loops of the nozzle here, at the outlet

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:55.440
of the turbine exhaust manifold of the low
pressure fuel pump.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:00.909
Liquid oxygen does not pass through this regenerative
cooling system and thus needed to be converted

00:22:00.909 --> 00:22:03.650
into a gas to pressurize the tanks.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:08.529
It instead was tapped off here at the outlet
of the main oxygen turbopump after passing

00:22:08.529 --> 00:22:12.509
through a heat exchanger around the turbine
of this pump.

00:22:12.509 --> 00:22:14.040
:
Finally there were electrical connections

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:19.350
on each plate too, made up of smaller electrical
wires that provided power to the external

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:24.390
tank and solid rocket boosters, while also
carrying vital telemetry data from each to

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:26.540
the orbiter's computers.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:31.799
Finally there was also liquid hydrogen recirculation
connection, which was only used during the

00:22:31.799 --> 00:22:36.559
liquid hydrogen engine chill down sequence
before launch, where the cryogenic fluid is

00:22:36.559 --> 00:22:41.530
circulated through the feed lines, valves
and turbopumps to bring them down to temperature.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:44.420
If this didn’t occur bubbles could form.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:47.710
The time for main engine cutoff is quickly
approaching.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:53.400
The process will be triggered by a predetermined
velocity target, around 28,000 kilometers

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:59.510
per hour, however it can be triggered by fuel
and oxidizer sensors detecting a low fluid

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:00.510
level.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:02.570
There are eight propellant depletion sensors.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:08.040
The four fuel depletion sensors are located
in the bottom of the liquid hydrogen tank.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:14.380
However, the liquid oxygen sensors were located
downstream of the feed line, inside the orbiter.

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:21.059
There was also an extra 500 kilograms of liquid
hydrogen loaded over the required 6:1 oxidiser/fuel

00:23:21.059 --> 00:23:22.490
mixture ratio.

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:28.020
Together these ensured that in the event the
main engine was cut off as a result of propellant

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:33.110
depletion, that main engine cut off occurred
in a fuel rich scenario.

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:38.340
As oxidizer rich engine shutdowns severely
erode and damage the reusable engines.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:39.340
[REF]

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:43.220
The time has now come for the main engines
to shut down and for the external tank to

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:44.659
separate.

00:23:44.659 --> 00:23:47.909
Frangible nuts once again explode to separate
the two structures.

00:23:47.909 --> 00:23:48.909
[Footage]

00:23:48.909 --> 00:23:53.290
The external tank begins to float away, leaving
the umbilical plates underneath the shuttle

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:57.070
exposed, and fixing that is the next challenge.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:58.840
you've got to close those doors.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:00.600
That's one of the first things you do.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:04.850
And that's very, very critical because if
you don't close those doors, you can't reenter

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:07.210
because you'll burn up during reentry.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:11.890
And so that's one of the things that we've
talked about, we've learned about, but nobody

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:17.340
has ever really thought about, or no one has
ever really come up with an easy solution.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:23.250
If you had to do an EVA to close those doors,
how you'd ever do it, you'd have to do a spacewalk

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.789
back to the back here somehow.

00:24:25.789 --> 00:24:31.820
And maybe hold on to the, there's just no
easy way to get underneath there to close

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:32.820
those.

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:36.059
But we talk about it, we come up with some
ideas, but

00:24:36.059 --> 00:24:37.270
Never had to be done.

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:43.179
There's never had to be done and there was
never a procedure set to do it.

00:24:43.179 --> 00:24:47.750
Thankfully these doors successfully closed
on every single space shuttle mission.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:52.130
The external tank is now on its way back to
earth's atmosphere, helped along by a vent

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:57.529
opening in the oxygen tank that causes the
tank to tumble away from the orbiter.

00:24:57.529 --> 00:25:02.039
With the thrust of the main engines gone the
astronauts have instantly become weightless,

00:25:02.039 --> 00:25:07.590
a serene moment that filled Bruce with so
much joy that he forgot his first job as a

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.039
rookie astronaut.

00:25:09.039 --> 00:25:15.630
Now, we always took a nice closeup picture
of the external tank right after Meco, because

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:20.410
if you'll remember, the external tank was
the only part of the space shuttle that wasn't

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:21.410
reused.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:25.590
The boosters would fly back or splash down
in the ocean, we'd tow them back in, refurbish

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.270
'em, the shuttle would come back.

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:32.440
But the external tank, as soon as we got off
the tank, it would start tumbling back to

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:36.810
earth and break up and burn up, reentering
the atmosphere and just splash down little

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.039
pieces.

00:25:38.039 --> 00:25:42.390
So we always wanted to take a nice big closeup
picture of the tank right after we got off

00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:47.620
it to see if there was any abnormality with
it that might be generic that we'd need to

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:50.260
fix before the next shuttle took off.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:55.940
So my very first job as a rookie was to, as
soon as we got to miko instantaneously, zero

00:25:55.940 --> 00:26:02.760
G was to get unstrapped out of my seat, float
down to a locker by me, get the proper camera

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:07.399
out, float up to the overhead window while
Dick Richards the commander on my first flight,

00:26:07.399 --> 00:26:11.430
was pitching discovery over on its back so
that we could take a picture of this tank

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:13.080
as it's following away back to earth.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:17.980
Well, I floated up to the overhead window
and I'm looking outside and Dick says, Hey

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:19.740
Bruce, you should be seeing it.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:21.149
And I said, yeah, Dick, I do.

00:26:21.149 --> 00:26:22.149
It's awesome.

00:26:22.149 --> 00:26:23.149
It's beautiful.

00:26:23.149 --> 00:26:24.370
He said, well, why aren't you taking any pictures?

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:25.640
I said, pictures of what?

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:26.640
Oh, the tank.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:32.940
I was so blown away by seeing Earth for the
first time from the space shuttle that I choked

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:36.730
on my first job and I was supposed to have
this great big picture of the tank.

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:40.789
Well, by the time I took a picture of the
tank, it was like falling away from us, but

00:26:40.789 --> 00:26:42.330
it was just beautiful.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:46.940
It was just seeing the earth for the first
time from space is probably my most memorable

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:47.940
experience.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:48.940
This is just the first stage of the incredible
engineering of the Space Shuttle.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:49.940
This was a reusable spacecraft designed to
provide the astronauts all the tools they

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:50.940
need to survive and work in orbit, including
sleeping quarters, life support, airlocks

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:51.940
and orbital maneuvering systems.

